{
  "description": "#4667 — READ-command differential over `get_backlinks`, the one of the three backlink candidates the #3347 allowlist labelled 'fixture candidate: not written yet' that the row projection can express (the two GROUPED siblings answer under `groups[].blocks`; see their allowlist entries). The backend reads the materialized `block_links` table and answers `ORDER BY bl.source_id ASC` under an `{id}` keyset (`agaric-store/src/pagination/links.rs`); the mock rescans block CONTENT for `[[ULID]]` tokens (`handlers/links.ts`). Three decisions have to agree for the two to land on the same page: which edges exist (S5 linked and then stopped, so a stale edge set serves it), the source-id ordering, and the `limit` + cursor keyset. Every linking block is a SEED block edited by an op, so its id is byte-identical on both stacks and the sort order is comparable; the link TOKENS arrive via `edit_block` because the seed insert bypasses the materializer and would leave `block_links` empty on the backend. NOT pinned, and deliberately: (a) the space filter — the harness has one space, so `active` and `global` select the same rows and `backlinks_global_scope` pins only that `Global` is served unscoped rather than refused the way `list_trash` refuses it; (b) the `b.deleted_at IS NULL` join filter, which needs a soft-deleted block still holding a link token — that state reddens the SNAPSHOT leg, because `DeleteBlock` enqueues no `ReindexBlockLinks` (`src/reconciliation_oracle.rs`) so the backend keeps the tombstoned source's `block_links` row by design, while the mock's `deriveLinkEdges` skips deleted sources outright. That is a snapshot-projection divergence in the mock, not a `get_backlinks` one, and fixing it means widening `deriveLinkEdges` and filtering at its two counting consumers instead.",
